{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c82d","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07545-b24a-7111-bd71-a49bf0fc5a97"},"sequence":625,"recorded_at":1780531400001245,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"e61bb3fc-6388-408f-a265-e94b890e4ba1","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"e61bb3fc-6388-408f-a265-e94b890e4ba1","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"e61bb3fc-6388-408f-a265-e94b890e4ba1"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"1e58e78f-1747-462d-9932-28dfc0e375ce"},"task_id":"1e58e78f-1747-462d-9932-28dfc0e375ce","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"1e58e78f-1747-462d-9932-28dfc0e375ce","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/STORY-260830-3drr2m/worktree/internal/sessadapter/discovery.go`.\n1|package sessadapter\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/scalar\"\n5|)\n6|\n7|// This file owns Session Adapter discovery. The adapter is not a\n8|// separately discovered plugin: it runs in the same trusted\n9|// ax-provider-<id> executable and under the same host-observed\n10|// executable digest as Provider Protocol 2.0.0. Discovering it\n11|// therefore means binding the adapter manifest the executable\n12|// returned to the independently observed provider candidate —\n13|// same provider identifier, same executable, same digests — and\n14|// sealing that binding so every later call and target mutation can\n15|// be checked against freshly read facts and the Journal binding.\n16|\n17|// Role names one side of a SessionAdapterExecutionBinding. The role\n18|// is call-scoped: a source binding never authorizes a target call.\n19|type Role string\n20|\n21|// The closed role vocabulary.\n22|const (\n23|\tRoleSource Role = \"source\"\n24|\tRoleTarget Role = \"target\"\n25|)\n26|\n27|// roleNames is the closed role vocabulary as a table so the census\n28|// derives it like every other closed vocabulary: Discover compares\n29|// through validRole, never through an inline comparison chain the\n30|// census cannot see. The table is built from the constants above,\n31|// so the two spellings cannot drift.\n32|var roleNames = []string{\n33|\tstring(RoleSource),\n34|\tstring(RoleTarget),\n35|}\n36|\n37|// validRole reports whether the role is one of the two closed\n38|// binding roles.\n39|func validRole(role Role) bool {\n40|\tfor _, allowed := range roleNames {\n41|\t\tif string(role) == allowed {\n42|\t\t\treturn true\n43|\t\t}\n44|\t}\n45|\treturn false\n46|}\n47|\n48|// TrustedCandidate is the independently observed provider\n49|// candidate the adapter must share: the identifier, kind,\n50|// executable path, owner identity, and digests the host observed\n51|// through provider discovery and trust, never through the adapter.\n52|type TrustedCandidate struct {\n53|\tProviderID             string\n54|\tKind                   CandidateKind\n55|\tExecutablePath         string\n56|\tOwnerIdentity          string\n57|\tExecutableSHA256       string\n58|\tProviderManifestDigest string\n59|}\n60|\n61|// ExecutionBinding is one sealed\n62|// SessionAdapterExecutionBinding: role, provider, candidate kind,\n63|// canonical executable path, owner identity, executable digest,\n64|// provider and adapter manifest digests, and the observation time.\n65|// verified_at records when the host observed these facts, not what\n66|// they are: equality compares the eight identity facts, never the\n67|// clock reading, because two reads of one unchanged candidate\n68|// legitimately carry different timestamps.\n69|type ExecutionBinding struct {\n70|\tRole                   Role\n71|\tProviderID             string\n72|\tCandidateKind          CandidateKind\n73|\tExecutablePath         string\n74|\tOwnerIdentity          string\n75|\tExecutableSHA256       string\n76|\tProviderManifestDigest string\n77|\tAdapterManifestDigest  string\n78|\tVerifiedAt             string\n79|}\n80|\n81|// Discover binds one validated adapter manifest to one trusted\n82|// candidate: the manifest's provider identifier must equal the\n83|// candidate's, and the host-computed manifest digest is sealed into\n84|// the binding alongside the candidate's executable facts. A\n85|// well-formed manifest for a different provider is an integrity\n86|// failure, not a usable adapter: agreement with the trusted\n87|// candidate is what makes the manifest this adapter's, and a\n88|// failed or partial observation never means the candidate is\n89|// absent.\n90|func Discover(role Role, manifest Manifest, manifestDigest string, candidate TrustedCandidate) (ExecutionBinding, error) {\n91|\tif !validRole(role) {\n92|\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"discovery role is outside source|target\", \"role\")\n93|\t\tif err != nil {\n94|\t\t\treturn ExecutionBinding{}, err\n95|\t\t}\n96|\t\treturn ExecutionBinding{}, failure\n97|\t}\n98|\tif manifest.ProviderID != candidate.ProviderID {\n99|\t\tfailure, err := failIntegrity(\"adapter manifest provider identifier does not match the trusted candidate\", \"provider_id\")\n100|\t\tif err != nil {\n101|\t\t\treturn ExecutionBinding{}, err\n102|\t\t}\n103|\t\treturn ExecutionBinding{}, failure\n104|\t}\n105|\tif candidate.ExecutablePath == \"\" {\n106|\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"discovery candidate carries no executable path\", \"executable_path\")\n107|\t\tif err != nil {\n108|\t\t\treturn ExecutionBinding{}, err\n109|\t\t}\n110|\t\treturn ExecutionBinding{}, failure\n111|\t}\n112|\tif candidate.OwnerIdentity == \"\" {\n113|\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"discovery candidate carries no owner identity\", \"owner_identity\")\n114|\t\tif err != nil {\n115|\t\t\treturn ExecutionBinding{}, err\n116|\t\t}\n117|\t\treturn ExecutionBinding{}, failure\n118|\t}\n119|\tif _, ok := checkDigestString(candidate.ExecutableSHA256); !ok {\n120|\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"discovery candidate executable digest is not a digest\", \"executable_sha256\")\n121|\t\tif err != nil {\n122|\t\t\treturn ExecutionBinding{}, err\n123|\t\t}\n124|\t\treturn ExecutionBinding{}, failure\n125|\t}\n126|\tif _, ok := checkDigestString(candidate.ProviderManifestDigest); !ok {\n127|\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"discovery candidate provider manifest digest is not a digest\", \"provider_manifest_digest\")\n128|\t\tif err != nil {\n129|\t\t\treturn ExecutionBinding{}, err\n130|\t\t}\n131|\t\treturn ExecutionBinding{}, failure\n132|\t}\n133|\tif _, ok := checkDigestString(manifestDigest); !ok {\n134|\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"discovery adapter manifest digest is not a digest\", \"session_adapter_manifest_digest\")\n135|\t\tif err != nil {\n136|\t\t\treturn ExecutionBinding{}, err\n137|\t\t}\n138|\t\treturn ExecutionBinding{}, failure\n139|\t}\n140|\treturn ExecutionBinding{\n141|\t\tRole:                   role,\n142|\t\tProviderID:             candidate.ProviderID,\n143|\t\tCandidateKind:          candidate.Kind,\n144|\t\tExecutablePath:         candidate.ExecutablePath,\n145|\t\tOwnerIdentity:          candidate.OwnerIdentity,\n146|\t\tExecutableSHA256:       candidate.ExecutableSHA256,\n147|\t\tProviderManifestDigest: candidate.ProviderManifestDigest,\n148|\t\tAdapterManifestDigest:  manifestDigest,\n149|\t\tVerifiedAt:             \"\",\n150|\t}, nil\n151|}\n152|\n153|// checkDigestString reports whether the value parses as a digest of\n154|// the pinned form.\n155|func checkDigestString(value string) (string, bool) {\n156|\tdigest, err := scalar.ParseDigest(value)\n157|\tif err != nil {\n158|\t\treturn \"\", false\n159|\t}\n160|\treturn digest.String(), true\n161|}\n162|\n163|// CheckBindingEquality requires the sealed binding to equal freshly\n164|// read trusted-candidate facts and the Journal binding on all\n165|// eight identity facts. Before every call and every target\n166|// mutation these facts MUST agree; a failed or partial read is an\n167|// integrity failure, and no self-claim or publisher claim\n168|// establishes trust. verified_at is compared by presence only\n169|// when both sides carry it: it timestamps the observation and two\n170|// observations of one unchanged candidate legitimately differ.\n171|func CheckBindingEquality(sealed, fresh ExecutionBinding) error {\n172|\tif sealed.Role != fresh.Role ||\n173|\t\tsealed.ProviderID != fresh.ProviderID ||\n174|\t\tsealed.CandidateKind != fresh.CandidateKind ||\n175|\t\tsealed.ExecutablePath != fresh.ExecutablePath ||\n176|\t\tsealed.OwnerIdentity != fresh.OwnerIdentity ||\n177|\t\tsealed.ExecutableSHA256 != fresh.ExecutableSHA256 ||\n178|\t\tsealed.ProviderManifestDigest != fresh.ProviderManifestDigest ||\n179|\t\tsealed.AdapterManifestDigest != fresh.AdapterManifestDigest {\n180|\t\tfailure, err := failIntegrity(\"session adapter binding no longer equals the freshly read trusted facts\", \"binding\")\n181|\t\tif err != nil {\n182|\t\t\treturn err\n183|\t\t}\n184|\t\treturn failure\n185|\t}\n186|\treturn nil\n187|}\n188|\n189|// CheckCallBinding requires one state-bearing call's context to\n190|// match its sealed binding and its admitted tuple: the provider\n191|// identifier, both digests, and the environment must equal the\n192|// binding facts and the tuple the registry admitted, and the role\n193|// must equal the call's side. A context answering for another\n194|// provider, another executable, another manifest, or an\n195|// unadmitted tuple is refused before the adapter surface is\n196|// touched.\n197|func CheckCallBinding(binding ExecutionBinding, role Role, context CallContext, admitted Tuple) error {\n198|\tif role != binding.Role {\n199|\t\tfailure, err := failIntegrity(\"adapter call role does not match the sealed binding role\", \"role\")\n200|\t\tif err != nil {\n201|\t\t\treturn err\n202|\t\t}\n203|\t\treturn failure\n204|\t}\n205|\tif context.ProviderID != binding.ProviderID {\n206|\t\tfailure, err := failIntegrity(\"adapter call provider identifier does not match the sealed binding\", \"provider_id\")\n207|\t\tif err != nil {\n208|\t\t\treturn err\n209|\t\t}\n210|\t\treturn failure\n211|\t}\n212|\tif context.ManifestDigest != binding.AdapterManifestDigest {\n213|\t\tfailure, err := failIntegrity(\"adapter call manifest digest does not match the sealed binding\", \"session_adapter_manifest_digest\")\n214|\t\tif err != nil {\n215|\t\t\treturn err\n216|\t\t}\n217|\t\treturn failure\n218|\t}\n219|\tif context.ExecutableSHA256 != binding.ExecutableSHA256 {\n220|\t\tfailure, err := failIntegrity(\"adapter call executable digest does not match the sealed binding\", \"executable_sha256\")\n221|\t\tif err != nil {\n222|\t\t\treturn err\n223|\t\t}\n224|\t\treturn failure\n225|\t}\n226|\tif context.Environment != admitted {\n227|\t\tfailure, err := failUnsupportedTuple(\"adapter call environment is not the admitted tuple\", context.Environment.EnvironmentID)\n228|\t\tif err != nil {\n229|\t\t\treturn err\n230|\t\t}\n231|\t\treturn failure\n232|\t}\n233|\treturn nil\n234|}"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c82f","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07545-b24a-7111-bd71-a49bf0fc5a97"},"sequence":626,"recorded_at":1780531400001247,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"e61bb3fc-6388-408f-a265-e94b890e4ba1","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.completed","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"e61bb3fc-6388-408f-a265-e94b890e4ba1","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"e61bb3fc-6388-408f-a265-e94b890e4ba1"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"1e58e78f-1747-462d-9932-28dfc0e375ce"},"task_id":"1e58e78f-1747-462d-9932-28dfc0e375ce","event":{"kind":"completed","task_id":"1e58e78f-1747-462d-9932-28dfc0e375ce"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c831","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07545-b24a-7111-bd71-a49bf0fc5a97"},"sequence":627,"recorded_at":1780531400001249,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"e61bb3fc-6388-408f-a265-e94b890e4ba1","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"e61bb3fc-6388-408f-a265-e94b890e4ba1","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"e61bb3fc-6388-408f-a265-e94b890e4ba1"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"0cf70521-ca38-46e3-959e-d2f3b0a9b45b"},"task_id":"0cf70521-ca38-46e3-959e-d2f3b0a9b45b","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"0cf70521-ca38-46e3-959e-d2f3b0a9b45b","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md`.\n1|---\n2|name: go-testing-tools\n3|description: >\n4|  Go testing toolkit with tuitestkit library and a closed-loop development\n5|  cycle for Go applications. Covers test helpers, reducer tests,\n6|  snapshot/golden testing, mock executor patterns, and view assertions.\n7|triggers:\n8|  - go test\n9|  - bubbletea test\n10|  - tuitestkit\n11|  - go test helpers\n12|  - reducer test\n13|  - snapshot test go\n14|  - golden file go\n15|  - тесты го\n16|  - го тест хелперы\n17|  - тесты баблти\n18|  - тест утилиты го\n19|  - замкнутый цикл тестирования\n20|---\n21|\n22|# Go Testing Tools\n23|\n24|Testing toolkit for Go TUI applications built with bubbletea. Provides a Go library (`tuitestkit`), reference documentation, and templates that enable a closed-loop development cycle: write code, write tests, run tests, validate, fix, repeat -- all without human intervention.\n25|\n26|Core philosophy: **Elm architecture makes TUI testable.** The reducer is a pure function -- given the same state and action, it always returns the same result. No I/O, no randomness, no terminal. Test everything as function calls.\n27|\n28|---\n29|\n30|## Prerequisites\n31|\n32|- **Go 1.21+**\n33|- **bubbletea** (`github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea`)\n34|- **lipgloss** (`github.com/charmbracelet/lipgloss`) -- used by most bubbletea apps for styling\n35|\n36|Install the library in your project:\n37|\n38|```bash\n39|go get github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit\n40|```\n41|\n42|---\n43|\n44|## Quick Start\n45|\n46|Minimal test showing the core workflow: build a message, send it to a model, assert on the view.\n47|\n48|```go\n49|package myscreen_test\n50|\n51|import (\n52|    \"testing\"\n53|\n54|    \"github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit\"\n55|)\n56|\n57|func TestBasicNavigation(t *testing.T) {\n58|    m := NewMyModel(testData)\n59|    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.WindowSize(80, 24))\n60|\n61|    // Press down twice\n62|    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.Key(\"down\"), tuitestkit.Key(\"down\"))\n63|\n64|    // Assert the view contains expected content\n65|    tuitestkit.ViewContains(t, m, \"third item\")\n66|    tuitestkit.ViewNotContains(t, m, \"ERROR\")\n67|}\n68|```\n69|\n70|---\n71|\n72|## Testing Pyramid\n73|\n74|Five levels, from fastest/most numerous to slowest/fewest:\n75|\n76|| Level | What | Speed | Investment |\n77||-------|------|-------|------------|\n78|| 1. Pure Reducer | `func(State, Action) State` | Nanoseconds | ~60% |\n79|| 2. Component | `Update` + `View` | Microseconds | ~20% |\n80|| 3. Integration | Multi-component with mocks | Milliseconds | ~10% |\n81|| 4. Snapshot | Golden file comparison | Milliseconds | ~5% |\n82|| 5. Behavioral | Full user workflows | Milliseconds | ~5% |\n83|\n84|When time is limited: **Level 1 > Level 2 > Level 3**. Pure reducer tests catch the most bugs per line of test code.\n85|\n86|Full details: `references/testing-pyramid.md`\n87|\n88|---\n89|\n90|## Library API Reference\n91|\n92|All functions are in the `tuitestkit` package.\n93|\n94|### Message Builders (`messages.go`)\n95|\n96|Build `tea.Msg` values for driving model updates.\n97|\n98|```go\n99|// Key builds a tea.KeyMsg from a human-readable string.\n100|// Supports: \"enter\", \"tab\", \"esc\", \"space\", \"backspace\", \"up\", \"down\",\n101|// \"left\", \"right\", \"home\", \"end\", \"pgup\", \"pgdown\", \"delete\", \"insert\",\n102|// \"f1\"-\"f20\", \"ctrl+c\", \"ctrl+a\"...\"ctrl+z\", \"alt+h\", \"alt+enter\",\n103|// single runes: \"a\", \"/\", \"1\", etc.\n104|func Key(k string) tea.KeyMsg\n105|\n106|// Keys builds a slice of tea.Msg from multiple key strings.\n107|func Keys(keys ...string) []tea.Msg\n108|\n109|// WindowSize builds a tea.WindowSizeMsg with the given dimensions.\n110|func WindowSize(w, h int) tea.WindowSizeMsg\n111|\n112|// MouseClick builds a tea.MouseMsg for a left-button click at (x, y).\n113|func MouseClick(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n114|\n115|// MouseClickRight builds a tea.MouseMsg for a right-button click at (x, y).\n116|func MouseClickRight(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n117|\n118|// MouseScroll builds a tea.MouseMsg for a scroll event.\n119|// Direction constants: ScrollUp, ScrollDown, ScrollLeft, ScrollRight.\n120|func MouseScroll(dir ScrollDir) tea.MouseMsg\n121|\n122|// MouseRelease builds a tea.MouseMsg for a button release at (x, y).\n123|func MouseRelease(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n124|```\n125|\n126|### Model Harness (`harness.go`)\n127|\n128|Send messages to bubbletea models and handle commands.\n129|\n130|```go\n131|// Send sends messages to a model sequentially, returns the final model.\n132|// Preserves concrete type via generics -- no type assertion needed.\n133|// Panics if Update returns a different type (broken implementation).\n134|// Init() is NOT called.\n135|func Send[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) M\n136|\n137|// SendAndCollect sends messages, collects all non-nil Cmds returned by Update.\n138|// Returns final model + collected commands.\n139|func SendAndCollect[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) (M, []tea.Cmd)\n140|\n141|// ExecCmds executes tea.Cmd functions synchronously, collects resulting messages.\n142|// Recursively handles tea.BatchMsg. Nil cmds/messages are skipped.\n143|func ExecCmds(cmds ...tea.Cmd) []tea.Msg\n144|```\n145|\n146|**Common pattern -- command pipeline:**\n147|\n148|```go\n149|m, cmds := tuitestkit.SendAndCollect(m, tuitestkit.Key(\"r\"))\n150|msgs := tuitestkit.ExecCmds(cmds...)\n151|m = tuitestkit.Send(m, msgs...)\n152|```\n153|\n154|### Reducer Harness (`reducer.go`)\n155|\n156|Table-driven testing for pure reducer functions.\n157|\n158|```go\n159|// ReducerTest defines a single test case for a pure reducer.\n160|// S = state type, A = action type.\n161|type ReducerTest[S any, A any] struct {\n162|    Name    string\n163|    Initial S\n164|    Action  A\n165|    Assert  func(t *testing.T, got S)\n166|}\n167|\n168|// Step defines one step in a multi-action reducer sequence.\n169|// Assert is optional -- if nil, applied without per-step validation.\n170|type Step[S any, A any] struct {\n171|    Name   string\n172|    Action A\n173|    Assert func(t *testing.T, got S)\n174|}\n175|\n176|// ReducerSequence defines a multi-step test scenario.\n177|// Actions applied sequentially from Initial. Final runs on end state.\n178|type ReducerSequence[S, A any] struct {\n179|    Name    string\n180|    Initial S\n181|    Steps   []Step[S, A]\n182|    Final   func(t *testing.T, got S)\n183|}\n184|\n185|// RunReducerTests executes table-driven reducer tests as subtests.\n186|func RunReducerTests[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, tests []ReducerTest[S, A])\n187|\n188|// RunReducerSequences executes multi-step sequence tests.\n189|// Per-step Assert runs after each step (if non-nil). Final runs on end state.\n190|func RunReducerSequences[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, sequences []ReducerSequence[S, A])\n191|```\n192|\n193|**Invariant checking:**\n194|\n195|```go\n196|// Invariant defines a property that must hold for any state.\n197|type Invariant[S any] struct {\n198|    Name  string\n199|    Check func(s S) error\n200|}\n201|\n202|// InvariantChecker holds invariants and validates state against all of them.\n203|type InvariantChecker[S any] struct { /* ... */ }\n204|\n205|// NewInvariantChecker creates a checker with the given invariants.\n206|func NewInvariantChecker[S any](invariants ...Invariant[S]) *InvariantChecker[S]\n207|\n208|// Check validates state against all invariants. Returns combined error or nil.\n209|func (ic *InvariantChecker[S]) Check(s S) error\n210|\n211|// WrapWithInvariants wraps a reducer with invariant checking.\n212|// After every reduce call, all invariants are validated.\n213|// t.Fatalf on violation.\n214|func WrapWithInvariants[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, checker *InvariantChecker[S]) func(S, A) S\n215|```\n216|\n217|**Example:**\n218|\n219|```go\n220|checker := tuitestkit.NewInvariantChecker(\n221|    tuitestkit.Invariant[AppState]{\n222|        Name: \"cursor in bounds\",\n223|        Check: func(s AppState) error {\n224|            if s.Cursor < 0 || s.Cursor >= len(s.Items) {\n225|                return fmt.Errorf(\"cursor %d out of [0, %d)\", s.Cursor, len(s.Items))\n226|            }\n227|            return nil\n228|        },\n229|    },\n230|)\n231|safeReduce := tuitestkit.WrapWithInvariants(t, Reduce, checker)\n232|tuitestkit.RunReducerTests(t, safeReduce, tests)\n233|```\n234|\n235|### Mock Building Blocks (`mock.go`)\n236|\n237|Composable primitives for building project-specific mocks.\n238|\n239|```go\n240|// MockCall represents a single recorded method invocation.\n241|type MockCall struct {\n242|    Method string\n243|    Args   []any\n244|}\n245|\n246|// MockCallRecorder provides thread-safe recording of method calls.\n247|// Embed into project-specific mock structs.\n248|type MockCallRecorder struct { /* ... */ }\n249|\n250|func (r *MockCallRecorder) Record(method string, args ...any)\n251|func (r *MockCallRecorder) CallCount(method string) int\n252|func (r *MockCallRecorder) Calls() []MockCall\n253|func (r *MockCallRecorder) CallsFor(method string) []MockCall\n254|func (r *MockCallRecorder) Reset()\n255|\n256|// MockResponseMap provides thread-safe canned response storage.\n257|type MockResponseMap struct { /* ... */ }\n258|\n259|func NewMockResponseMap() *MockResponseMap\n260|func (m *MockResponseMap) Set(key string, data []byte, err error)\n261|func (m *MockResponseMap) Get(key string) ([]byte, error)\n262|func (m *MockResponseMap) SetError(key string, err error)\n263|\n264|// Assertion helpers\n265|func AssertCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\n266|func AssertNotCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\n267|func AssertCalledN(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, n int)\n268|func AssertCalledWith(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, args ...any)\n269|```\n270|\n271|**Building a mock (two lines per method):**\n272|\n273|```go\n274|type TaskBoardMock struct {\n275|    tuitestkit.MockCallRecorder\n276|    Responses *tuitestkit.MockResponseMap\n277|}\n278|\n279|func NewTaskBoardMock() *TaskBoardMock {\n280|    return &TaskBoardMock{Responses: tuitestkit.NewMockResponseMap()}\n281|}\n282|\n283|func (m *TaskBoardMock) TreeJSON() ([]byte, error) {\n284|    m.Record(\"TreeJSON\")\n285|    return m.Responses.Get(\"TreeJSON\")\n286|}\n287|\n288|func (m *TaskBoardMock) Execute(cmd string, args ...string) ([]byte, error) {\n289|    m.Record(\"Execute\", cmd, args)\n290|    return m.Responses.Get(\"Execute:\" + cmd)\n291|}\n292|```\n293|\n294|### View Assertions (`view.go`)\n295|\n296|Assert on rendered output. All functions strip ANSI escape codes before comparison.\n297|\n298|```go\n299|// StripANSI removes all ANSI escape sequences from a string.\n300|func StripANSI(s string) string\n301|\n302|// --- Model-based (call model.View() internally) ---\n303|\n304|func ViewContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, text string)\n305|func ViewNotContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, text string)\n306|func ViewLines(model tea.Model) []string\n307|func ViewLineContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, lineIdx int, text string)\n308|func ViewLineEquals(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, lineIdx int, text string)\n309|func ViewMatchesRegex(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, pattern string)\n310|\n311|// --- String-based (when you already have the view string) ---\n312|\n313|func ContainsStr(t testing.TB, view string, text string)\n314|func NotContainsStr(t testing.TB, view string, text string)\n315|func LinesFromStr(view string) []string\n316|func MatchesRegexStr(t testing.TB, view string, pattern string)\n317|```\n318|\n319|### Snapshot Testing (`snapshot.go`)\n320|\n321|Golden file comparison for visual regression testing.\n322|\n323|```go\n324|// UpdateSnapshots controls whether snapshots are written (true) or compared (false).\n325|// Set via UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 environment variable.\n326|var UpdateSnapshots bool\n327|\n328|// SnapshotView captures model.View(), strips ANSI, compares against golden file.\n329|func SnapshotView(t *testing.T, model tea.Model, name string)\n330|\n331|// SnapshotViewRaw captures model.View() with raw ANSI codes intact.\n332|func SnapshotViewRaw(t *testing.T, model tea.Model, name string)\n333|\n334|// SnapshotStr compares a pre-rendered string (ANSI-stripped) against golden file.\n335|func SnapshotStr(t *testing.T, view string, name string)\n336|\n337|// SnapshotStrRaw compares a pre-rendered string (raw ANSI) against golden file.\n338|func SnapshotStrRaw(t *testing.T, view string, name string)\n339|```\n340|\n341|Golden files stored at `testdata/snapshots/<name>.golden` relative to the test file.\n342|\n343|```bash\n344|# Create or update golden files\n345|UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 go test ./...\n346|\n347|# Update for a single package\n348|UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 go test ./internal/ui/screens/board/ -run TestSnapshot\n349|```\n350|\n351|Mismatches produce a line-by-line diff with `-`/`+` markers.\n352|\n353|---\n354|\n355|## Closed-Loop Workflow\n356|\n357|The agent development cycle for writing tests:\n358|\n359|```\n360|1. UNDERSTAND: Read screen/component code\n361|2. IDENTIFY:   What behaviors need tests?\n362|3. EXTRACT:    If exec.Command present, extract to interface (use mock template)\n363|4. WRITE:      Tests using tuitestkit helpers\n364|5. RUN:        go test ./... -v\n365|6. VALIDATE:   All pass? Check coverage with go test -cover\n366|7. FIX:        If failures, read error, fix code or test, goto 5\n367|8. SNAPSHOT:   Optionally capture view golden files\n368|9. DONE:       Tests green, coverage acceptable\n369|```\n370|\n371|### Step Details\n372|\n373|**UNDERSTAND:** Read the component source. Identify:\n374|- What state does it manage?\n375|- What messages does it handle in Update?\n376|- Does it have a pure reducer (`func(State, Action) State`)?\n377|- Does it shell out to external commands (exec.Command)?\n378|- What does View() render?\n379|\n380|**IDENTIFY:** Map behaviors to test levels:\n381|- Pure state logic -> Level 1 (reducer tests)\n382|- Key bindings, view rendering -> Level 2 (component tests)\n383|- Data loading, command execution -> Level 3 (integration tests with mocks)\n384|- Visual appearance -> Level 4 (snapshot tests)\n385|\n386|**EXTRACT:** If the component uses `exec.Command` or any I/O directly:\n387|1. Define an interface with only the methods this component needs\n388|2. Move `exec.Command` calls to a production implementation\n389|3. Build a mock using `MockCallRecorder` + `MockResponseMap`\n390|4. See `assets/templates/executor_interface.go.tmpl` and `assets/templates/mock_executor.go.tmpl`\n391|\n392|**WRITE:** Use the appropriate tuitestkit helpers per level:\n393|- Level 1: `RunReducerTests`, `RunReducerSequences`, `WrapWithInvariants`\n394|- Level 2: `Send`, `ViewContains`, `ViewLines`, `Key`, `MouseClick`\n395|- Level 3: `SendAndCollect`, `ExecCmds`, `AssertCalled`, `AssertCalledWith`\n396|- Level 4: `SnapshotView`, `SnapshotStr`\n397|\n398|**RUN:** `go test ./... -v` -- all tests must run in under 1 second (no I/O, no network).\n399|\n400|**VALIDATE:** `go test ./... -cover` -- target 80%+ for code under test.\n401|\n402|**FIX:** Read error messages carefully. Common issues:\n403|- Type assertion panic -> `Update` returns wrong type (broken implementation)\n404|- View assertion failure -> check ANSI stripping, check exact text\n405|- Snapshot mismatch -> run with `UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1` if the change is intentional\n406|- Mock assertion failure -> check response key matches (`\"Execute:list\"` not `\"Execute\"`)\n407|\n408|---\n409|\n410|## Templates\n411|\n412|Scaffolding templates in `assets/templates/`:\n413|\n414|| Template | Purpose |\n415||----------|---------|\n416|| `reducer_test.go.tmpl` | Table-driven reducer tests with invariants |\n417|| `component_test.go.tmpl` | Component model tests (keyboard, mouse, commands, view) |\n418|| `snapshot_test.go.tmpl` | Snapshot golden file tests |\n419|| `executor_interface.go.tmpl` | Executor interface + production stub |\n420|| `mock_executor.go.tmpl` | Mock executor using MockCallRecorder + MockResponseMap |\n421|| `PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md` | Recommended directory layout for test files |\n422|\n423|Copy templates into your project, replace `TODO` markers with your actual types and import paths.\n424|\n425|---\n426|\n427|## References\n428|\n429|In-depth documentation in `references/`:\n430|\n431|| Document | Content |\n432||----------|---------|\n433|| `elm-architecture.md` | Why Elm makes TUI testable, state/action/reducer/view cycle, nested reducers, dependency injection, the executor pattern |\n434|| `mock-patterns.md` | Interface extraction, when to mock vs test pure functions, stubs vs mocks vs fakes, callback capture, full mock lifecycle example |\n435|| `testing-pyramid.md` | All 5 test levels with code examples, decision table for which level to use, coverage strategy |\n436|\n437|---\n438|\n439|## Setup\n440|\n441|Run the setup script to create symlinks:\n442|\n443|```bash\n444|./scripts/setup.sh\n445|```\n446|\n447|This links the skill to `~/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools`, `~/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools`, and `~/.codex/skills/go-testing-tools`.\n448|\n449|To remove:\n450|\n451|```bash\n452|./scripts/deinit.sh\n453|```"}}}
